\title[Scan schemes and the $1/(2\varepsilon)$ compression barrier]
{Scan Schemes: Honest Uniqueness Decoding, Exact Cost Accounting,\\
and the $1/(2\varepsilon)$ Compression Barrier}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, exact analysis of the simplest and most widely deployed lookup
structure: a \emph{scan scheme}, in which $N$ totally ordered keys are distributed
among $m$ labelled buckets and a key is retrieved by scanning its bucket linearly.
Two foundational facts anchor the model. First, \emph{honest uniqueness decoding}:
the pair consisting of a key's bucket label and its intra-bucket index is a uniquely
decodable code for that key. Second, \emph{exact cost accounting}: the total decoding
cost of a scheme equals, identically, the sum over buckets of the triangular number
of the bucket's size. These reduce the optimisation of scan schemes to an arithmetic
problem which we solve exactly. Using an integral tangent-line inequality for
$T(k)=k(k+1)/2$ with the upper slope $q+1$, we prove that the least achievable total
cost of $N$ keys in $m$ buckets is $\Opt(N,m) = r\,T(q+1) + (m-r)\,T(q)$ with
$q=\lfloor N/m\rfloor$, $r = N \bmod m$, attained by the residue scheme
$x \mapsto x \bmod m$; that the optimum is \emph{rigid}, i.e.\ a scheme is optimal if
and only if every bucket has size $q$ or $q+1$; that the total cost is invariant under
the natural $\Sym(\text{keys})\times\Sym(\text{buckets})$ action; that unit cost
everywhere is equivalent to injectivity of the bucket map; that the greatest
achievable cost is $T(N)$; and that the mean decoding cost of every scheme is at least
$\tfrac12(N/m+1)$, giving the $\varepsilon$-compression barrier: a scheme using
$m \le \varepsilon N$ buckets has mean decoding cost at least $1/(2\varepsilon)$, with
the constant $1/2$ sharp. Finally we determine the achievable cost set exactly: it
lies in $[\Opt(N,m), T(N)]$ with both endpoints attained, but it is not the full
integer interval; it is the set of sums of triangular numbers over partitions of $N$
into at most $m$ parts, which for $(N,m)=(5,3)$ equals $\{7,8,9,11,15\}$.
\end{abstract}

